%[text] # Week 5 Practical Activity: Compare Root Finders for Kepler's Equation
%[text] **PRACTICAL ACTIVITY.** This group investigation assesses nonlinear model formulation, bracket construction, explicit bisection and Newton iteration, stopping criteria, residual validation, failure diagnosis, quantitative method comparison, and capstone-method selection. A trustworthy numerical argument matters more than a long program.
%[text] **Physical question.** How do bracketing, initial guess, eccentricity, and stopping evidence affect the reliability and efficiency of solving Kepler's equation $E-e\\sin(E)=M$?
%[text] **Working arrangement.** Complete the Week 05 Individual Practical Check — Quiz in Google Classroom before discussing the problem or using MATLAB, notes, calculators, or AI. Then work in a group of two or three. MATLAB Copilot may be used during group work, but every material suggestion must be declared and independently tested.
%%
%[text] ## Complete the Individual Check First
%[text] Each student submits the Week 05 Individual Practical Check — Quiz from Google Classroom before group work begins. The lecturer will confirm when the quiz is available. Do not duplicate those answers here.
%%
%[text] ## Group Record and Role Rotation
%[text] Record all members and assign model, implementation, and validation leads. Rotate roles from earlier practicals.
groupMembers = "TODO: names and matric numbers" %[output:802d1790]
roleRecord = "TODO: identify the model, implementation, and validation leads" %[output:1be0f430]
%%
%[text] ## Predict Before Computing
%[text] For $0\\le e\<1$, predict whether the Kepler residual $f(E)=E-e\\sin(E)-M$ is increasing or decreasing. Predict which baseline method will use fewer iterations, bisection or Newton from $E\_0=M$. Finally, predict what will happen to a Newton step if $|f'(E)|$ becomes very small.
predictionRecord = "TODO: monotonicity, expected iteration ordering, small-derivative effect, and physical/numerical reasons" %[output:32219dfc]
%%
%[text] ## State the Model, Variables, and Assumptions
%[text] Record the meaning and units of $E$, $M$, and $e$. State the elliptic-orbit restriction $0\\le e\<1$ and explain why a root of the residual is equivalent to satisfying Kepler's equation.
modelRecord = "TODO: variable meanings, radians/dimensionless units, elliptic-orbit assumption, and residual definition" %[output:4ad4bea0]
%%
%[text] ## Prove a Bracket Before Calling a Solver
%[text] Show analytically that $\[M-e,M+e\]$ brackets a root by evaluating the signs of $f(M-e)$ and $f(M+e)$. Then use $f'(E)=1-e\\cos(E)$ to decide whether the root is unique for $e\<1$. Record the logic before inspecting the numerical endpoint values below.
bracketProofRecord = "TODO: endpoint sign inequalities, continuity argument, derivative bound, and uniqueness conclusion" %[output:1ae76fdd]
%%
%[text] ## Evaluate the Baseline Physical Case
%[text] Use $e=0.70$ and $M=1.00,\\mathrm{rad}$. Construct the physical residual and its derivative, evaluate the analytic bracket, and verify the endpoint signs computationally.
eBaseline = 0.70;
MBaseline_rad = 1.00;
keplerResidual = @(E_rad) E_rad-eBaseline*sin(E_rad)-MBaseline_rad;
keplerDerivative = @(E_rad) 1-eBaseline*cos(E_rad);
lowerBracket_rad = MBaseline_rad-eBaseline;
upperBracket_rad = MBaseline_rad+eBaseline;
lowerResidual = keplerResidual(lowerBracket_rad);
upperResidual = keplerResidual(upperBracket_rad);
baselineBracketRecord = table(eBaseline,MBaseline_rad,lowerBracket_rad,lowerResidual,upperBracket_rad,upperResidual) %[output:601c15af]
assert(lowerResidual <= 0 && upperResidual >= 0,'The analytic Kepler bracket should contain a sign change or endpoint root.')
baselineInterpretation = "TODO: connect the numerical endpoint signs to the analytic existence and uniqueness argument" %[output:401d45cc]
%%
%[text] ## Run Explicit Bisection
%[text] The local `bisectRoot` function implements the midpoint-and-sign-update algorithm so the iteration is inspectable. It stops when the current half-width is at most `toleranceX*(1+abs(E))`. Because the root remains bracketed, that half-width is a certified root-location uncertainty for the midpoint estimate.
toleranceX = 1e-10;
maximumIterations = 80;
[rootBisection_rad,bisectionIterations,bisectionHistory] = bisectRoot(keplerResidual,lowerBracket_rad,upperBracket_rad,toleranceX,maximumIterations);
bisectionResidual = abs(keplerResidual(rootBisection_rad));
bisectionFinalHalfWidth_rad = bisectionHistory.HalfWidth_rad(end);
bisectionRecord = table(rootBisection_rad,bisectionIterations,bisectionResidual,bisectionFinalHalfWidth_rad) %[output:0ad39cc0]
assert(bisectionFinalHalfWidth_rad <= toleranceX*(1+abs(rootBisection_rad)),'Bisection should satisfy the stated interval-width rule.')
bisectionEvidence = "TODO: explain what the half-width guarantees, what the residual checks, and why they are not the same quantity" %[output:05dd3c4a]
%%
%[text] ## Run Explicit Newton Iteration
%[text] Newton uses $E\_{k+1}=E\_k-f(E\_k)/f'(E\_k)$. Start from the physically natural guess $E\_0=M$. The provided implementation does not declare convergence until **both** the scaled step and absolute residual satisfy their tolerances; it also detects a very small derivative.
initialNewton_rad = MBaseline_rad;
residualTolerance = 1e-12;
derivativeFloor = 1e-12;
[rootNewton_rad,newtonIterations,newtonHistory,newtonStatus] = newtonRoot(keplerResidual,keplerDerivative,initialNewton_rad,toleranceX,residualTolerance,maximumIterations,derivativeFloor);
newtonResidual = abs(keplerResidual(rootNewton_rad));
newtonRecord = table(rootNewton_rad,newtonIterations,newtonResidual,newtonStatus) %[output:8d37fd0f]
assert(newtonStatus == "converged",'Newton should converge for the baseline physical initial guess.')
newtonEvidence = "TODO: compare the observed iteration path with the prediction and explain why step plus residual is stronger than step alone" %[output:490b6bd0]
%%
%[text] ## Validate with MATLAB fzero
%[text] Use the same physical bracket with MATLAB `fzero`. Treat the built-in result as an independent implementation, not an oracle: inspect its exit flag, evaluate the defining residual yourself, and compare all three roots quantitatively.
fzeroOptions = optimset('TolX',toleranceX);
[rootFzero_rad,fzeroResidualSigned,fzeroExitFlag,fzeroOutput] = fzero(keplerResidual,[lowerBracket_rad upperBracket_rad],fzeroOptions);
fzeroResidual = abs(fzeroResidualSigned);
method = ["Bisection";"Newton";"fzero"];
root_rad = [rootBisection_rad;rootNewton_rad;rootFzero_rad];
iterations = [bisectionIterations;newtonIterations;fzeroOutput.iterations];
absoluteResidual = [bisectionResidual;newtonResidual;fzeroResidual];
baselineComparison = table(method,root_rad,iterations,absoluteResidual) %[output:22c17f7a]
assert(fzeroExitFlag > 0,'fzero should converge on the valid baseline bracket.')
assert(abs(rootBisection_rad-rootFzero_rad) <= bisectionFinalHalfWidth_rad+toleranceX,'The fzero reference should agree with the certified bisection interval.')
assert(abs(rootNewton_rad-rootFzero_rad) < 1e-9,'Newton and fzero should agree for the baseline case.')
comparisonRecord = "TODO: rank efficiency and robustness using iteration count, residual, bracket guarantee, initial-guess dependence, and solver status" %[output:26621e80]
%%
%[text] ## Plot the Convergence Evidence
%[text] Compare the absolute residual histories. Use the plot to describe convergence behaviour, but do not infer a root-location error directly from residual magnitude unless you also justify the local slope.
semilogy(bisectionHistory.Iteration,max(bisectionHistory.AbsResidual,eps),'-o','LineWidth',1.2) %[output:93cd8764]
hold on %[output:93cd8764]
semilogy(newtonHistory.Iteration,max(newtonHistory.AbsResidual,eps),'-s','LineWidth',1.2) %[output:93cd8764]
hold off %[output:93cd8764]
xlabel('Iteration') %[output:93cd8764]
ylabel('Absolute residual |f(E)| (rad)') %[output:93cd8764]
title('Baseline convergence evidence') %[output:93cd8764]
legend('Bisection','Newton','Location','best') %[output:93cd8764]
grid on %[output:93cd8764]
plotInterpretation = "TODO: describe the two convergence patterns without claiming that residual and root error are identical" %[output:61620683]
%%
%[text] ## Test an Invalid Bracket
%[text] Use the baseline residual on $\[0,0.1\]$. Do not call bisection if the endpoints have the same sign. Record why this is an input/model-evidence failure rather than a request for more iterations.
invalidLeft_rad = 0.0;
invalidRight_rad = 0.1;
invalidResidualLeft = keplerResidual(invalidLeft_rad);
invalidResidualRight = keplerResidual(invalidRight_rad);
invalidBracketDetected = invalidResidualLeft*invalidResidualRight > 0;
invalidBracketRecord = table(invalidLeft_rad,invalidResidualLeft,invalidRight_rad,invalidResidualRight) %[output:72a1f7d7]
assert(invalidBracketDetected,'The deliberately invalid interval should fail the sign-change test.')
invalidBracketDiagnosis = "TODO: defect, consequence, and how you would repair or re-justify the interval" %[output:9e5ebc07]
%%
%[text] ## Stress-Test Newton at High Eccentricity
%[text] Change to $e=0.999$ and $M=0.15,\\mathrm{rad}$. Deliberately use $E\_0=0$. Compute the derivative and first Newton step **before** running a full solver. Then explain why a $150,\\mathrm{rad}$ first candidate is numerically suspicious even though the physical root is near the original scale.
eStress = 0.999;
MStress_rad = 0.15;
stressResidual = @(E_rad) E_rad-eStress*sin(E_rad)-MStress_rad;
stressDerivative = @(E_rad) 1-eStress*cos(E_rad);
stressInitial_rad = 0.0;
stressDerivativeAtInitial = stressDerivative(stressInitial_rad);
stressFirstStep_rad = -stressResidual(stressInitial_rad)/stressDerivativeAtInitial;
stressFirstCandidate_rad = stressInitial_rad+stressFirstStep_rad;
stressStepRecord = table(eStress,MStress_rad,stressDerivativeAtInitial,stressFirstStep_rad,stressFirstCandidate_rad) %[output:670cc2ef]
assert(abs(stressFirstStep_rad) > 100,'The chosen stress case should produce a very large first Newton step.')
stressPredictionCheck = "TODO: compare the observed derivative and step with your prediction; explain the sensitivity mechanism" %[output:8e103972]
%%
%[text] ## Compare Raw and Safeguarded Behaviour
%[text] Run raw Newton from the poor initial guess, then solve the same problem with bisection and a safeguarded Newton method. The safeguarded method keeps the analytic bracket as an invariant: if a Newton candidate leaves the bracket or the derivative is unusable, it falls back to a midpoint.
stressLower_rad = MStress_rad-eStress;
stressUpper_rad = MStress_rad+eStress;
[stressRawRoot_rad,stressRawIterations,stressRawHistory,stressRawStatus] = newtonRoot(stressResidual,stressDerivative,stressInitial_rad,toleranceX,residualTolerance,maximumIterations,derivativeFloor);
[stressBisectionRoot_rad,stressBisectionIterations,stressBisectionHistory] = bisectRoot(stressResidual,stressLower_rad,stressUpper_rad,toleranceX,maximumIterations);
[stressGuardedRoot_rad,stressGuardedIterations,stressGuardedHistory,stressGuardedStatus] = guardedNewtonRoot(stressResidual,stressDerivative,stressInitial_rad,stressLower_rad,stressUpper_rad,toleranceX,residualTolerance,maximumIterations,derivativeFloor);
stressMethod = ["Raw Newton";"Bisection";"Guarded Newton"];
stressRoot_rad = [stressRawRoot_rad;stressBisectionRoot_rad;stressGuardedRoot_rad];
stressIterations = [stressRawIterations;stressBisectionIterations;stressGuardedIterations];
stressAbsResidual = [abs(stressResidual(stressRawRoot_rad));abs(stressResidual(stressBisectionRoot_rad));abs(stressResidual(stressGuardedRoot_rad))];
stressStatus = [stressRawStatus;"bracketed";stressGuardedStatus];
stressComparison = table(stressMethod,stressRoot_rad,stressIterations,stressAbsResidual,stressStatus) %[output:702e4fdb]
assert(stressGuardedStatus == "converged",'Safeguarded Newton should converge for the stress case.')
assert(abs(stressBisectionRoot_rad-stressGuardedRoot_rad) < 1e-8,'Bisection and safeguarded Newton should agree on the stress-case root.')
safeguardFallbackCount = sum(stressGuardedHistory.UsedBisectionFallback) %[output:88d8c1db]
stressDiagnosis = "TODO: compare path, iterations, residual, and robustness; state whether the raw method recovered and why that does not erase the warning from its first step" %[output:43291fcd]
%%
%[text] ## Investigate Eccentricity as a Controlled Parameter
%[text] Keep $M=0.15,\\mathrm{rad}$ and solve four elliptic cases. Use the physical bracket for bisection and $E\_0=M$ for Newton. Record how the minimum derivative bound $1-e$ shrinks as eccentricity approaches one, then decide whether iteration count alone is enough to judge method quality.
eccentricityValues = [0.10;0.70;0.95;0.999];
investigationM_rad = 0.15;
nCases = numel(eccentricityValues);
bisectionIterationCount = zeros(nCases,1);
newtonIterationCount = zeros(nCases,1);
bisectionResidualByCase = zeros(nCases,1);
newtonResidualByCase = zeros(nCases,1);
minimumDerivativeBound = 1-eccentricityValues;
for caseIndex = 1:nCases
    eCase = eccentricityValues(caseIndex);
    caseResidual = @(E_rad) E_rad-eCase*sin(E_rad)-investigationM_rad;
    caseDerivative = @(E_rad) 1-eCase*cos(E_rad);
    caseLeft_rad = investigationM_rad-eCase;
    caseRight_rad = investigationM_rad+eCase;
    [caseBisectionRoot_rad,bisectionIterationCount(caseIndex)] = bisectRoot(caseResidual,caseLeft_rad,caseRight_rad,toleranceX,maximumIterations);
    [caseNewtonRoot_rad,newtonIterationCount(caseIndex),~,caseNewtonStatus] = newtonRoot(caseResidual,caseDerivative,investigationM_rad,toleranceX,residualTolerance,maximumIterations,derivativeFloor);
    assert(caseNewtonStatus == "converged",'Newton from E0=M should converge for each controlled eccentricity case.')
    bisectionResidualByCase(caseIndex) = abs(caseResidual(caseBisectionRoot_rad));
    newtonResidualByCase(caseIndex) = abs(caseResidual(caseNewtonRoot_rad));
end
eccentricityInvestigation = table(eccentricityValues,minimumDerivativeBound,bisectionIterationCount,newtonIterationCount,bisectionResidualByCase,newtonResidualByCase) %[output:2e2a85be]
